{"connection_id": "BC-88bb1001cc005998cdb7", "ref": "1 Clement 24.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the seed sown in earth and its emergence as an image of resurrection", "note": "The commentary contrasts perishing pasture with the crop that remains; Clement points to seed put in earth and its subsequent growth as evidence for resurrection, connecting agricultural persistence with life beyond death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55b9c22ddecec1d4950c", "ref": "Basil of Caesarea, Homily 6 on Luke 12:18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "hoarded grain and the poor", "note": "Basil reads the rich man's larger barns as hoarding surplus that belongs with the needy; this Christian interpretation makes explicit the commentary's alternative between a harvest shared and one retained for self."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1e1ff59d228e0bace3d0", "ref": "Didache 13.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first fruits from threshing floor, wine press, oxen, and sheep", "note": "The commentary describes a share of produce being given; the Didache specifies first fruits from harvest and livestock for prophets, or for the poor if no prophet is present, making the due a concrete communal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-530d6e2f5c4be973e34a", "ref": "Didache 13:3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "firstfruits of winepress and threshing floor", "note": "The Didache directs firstfruits from winepress, threshing floor, cattle, and sheep to prophets; it carries the commentary's harvest-due motif into early Christian community order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0903cdedd9484074fefa", "ref": "Didache 13:4", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "firstfruits given to poor", "note": "If no prophet is present, the Didache directs firstfruits to the poor; it makes the crop share a relief obligation, not private purification alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-888e035944691dab69ae", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "seed dies in order to live", "note": "The commentary uses a seed smothered under soil as purification's opposite, whereas Paul makes a sown seed's death the necessary image of resurrection; the opposed agricultural valuation is the link."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f8786865cb75e8ce0a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives each seed its own body", "note": "The commentary attributes seed growth to divine blessing; Paul says God gives the seed its body as God wills, making the source of differentiated growth explicitly divine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02fd78ba0f7ad92c0662", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body sown perishable and raised imperishable", "note": "The commentary opposes perishing pasture to what remains; Paul uses sowing and resurrection to contrast perishable and imperishable bodies, extending the crop's persistence to resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17e428609bf881522f0c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one plants, another waters, God gives growth", "note": "The commentary's field needs cultivation and growth; Paul distinguishes planting and watering from God's giving the increase, locating the crop's growth in divine care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6fb849cbcde12b7b7e9", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "planting, watering, and God's growth", "note": "Paul plants, Apollos waters, but God gives the growth; this is an unusually exact divine growth sequence for the commentary's cultivated crop."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1816e73b7a79726d9e9a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God alone gives growth", "note": "Neither planter nor waterer is decisive, but God who gives growth; it identifies divine agency in the increase the commentary connects with blessing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7b964c6390c2f6a3bfd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's field and fellow workers", "note": "The commentary reads Rabb through tending a farm; Paul calls the community God's field and its ministers God's coworkers, turning stewardship of a field into a communal image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6c1f1003015a2cc687d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's cultivated field", "note": "Paul calls the community God's field; it converts the commentary's cared-for farm into a communal ethical image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59a068dc8fa96e3ba9a4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the plowman plows in hope and the thresher hopes to share the crop", "note": "The commentary names the farmer through breaking soil and gives the crop a due share; Paul says agricultural workers labor in hope of sharing the produce, directly joining cultivation and right to yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-539c91fbb64519889430", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vineyard planter eats its fruit", "note": "Paul asks who plants a vineyard without eating its fruit; it offers a distinct New Testament use of farm labor and a rightful portion beside the commentary's farmer and yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b87d043435596bcb98a0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all flesh is grass and its glory like a flower that fades", "note": "The commentary contrasts perishing pasture with lasting good; Peter applies Isaiah's grass-and-flower image to human life, making the pasture's decay a figure for mortal transience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3531124991ec019f974", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass dries and its flower falls", "note": "All flesh is grass whose flower falls when the grass dries; the formula closely matches the commentary's passing pasture image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5fe7dd3e5bb45027e3ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "the Lord's word remains forever and is the preached gospel", "note": "The commentary sets fading growth against what endures; Peter continues the grass quotation by identifying the enduring word with the gospel preached to the audience, giving Isaiah's plant contrast a Christian proclamation context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f2b5bf5cdef3edd73c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word remains forever", "note": "The Lord's word remains forever after the grass-and-flower contrast; it supplies an explicit enduring term beside the commentary's abqā."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b41155332893d4eaaf59", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "generosity and readiness to share", "note": "The wealthy are commanded to be generous and ready to share; it directly develops the commentary's wealth-share aspect outside the harvest image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1cf63e662102b163f6f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "generosity lays up a foundation for the future and real life", "note": "The commentary links paying the crop's due with what lasts; Timothy's instruction joins generosity to a future foundation and taking hold of life, giving the share an explicitly lasting horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a562e81d4000bd81344", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foundation for the future and true life", "note": "The result is a stored foundation for the future and grasping true life; it supplies a durable-life outcome for the generosity enjoined in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6969224573824b0af888", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God supplies seed and multiplies harvest of righteousness", "note": "Commentary links crop growth, giving, and purification; Paul says God supplies seed, increases it, and multiplies the harvest of righteousness, making generosity productive in righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ff6ff2e9fc32345a540", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.10", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seed, bread, harvest of righteousness", "note": "God supplies seed to the sower and bread for food, multiplies seed, and increases a harvest of righteousness; its gift context maps the commentary's blessed crop-growth to ethical fruit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-adbb4c8adf05f6a00fc6", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing generously and reaping generously", "note": "The commentary pictures a share given from growing produce; Paul makes generosity itself sowing whose return is measured at harvest, extending the crop image to giving."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab9ba1eac9c7becb9643", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving to poor; righteousness remains forever", "note": "Paul joins scattering gifts to the poor with righteousness remaining forever; it combines the commentary's paid crop-share with its falāḥ and bāqī permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b106432c68c90a7fe5fd", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hardworking farmer's first share of crops", "note": "The hard-working farmer ought first to receive a share of the crops; this provides a distinct New Testament use of cultivation, labor, and a rightful portion beside the commentary's farmer and yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eeca1ceb03e84c91e5c9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.17", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rains and fruitful seasons fill people with food and gladness", "note": "The commentary attributes growth to divine blessing; Paul points to God giving rain and fruitful seasons, joining crop provision with nourishment and joy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11b23912a208eeb1781f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing to the Spirit reaps eternal life", "note": "The commentary joins a cultivated yield with what is better and more enduring; Paul makes sowing to the Spirit yield eternal life, transferring the harvest to the afterlife."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ac57ef584a95be3ed62", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing to flesh or Spirit; corruption or eternal life", "note": "The two sowings culminate in corruption or eternal life; it gives direct field language to the commentary's contrast between a worldly crop and an afterlife-directed one."}
{"connection_id": "BC-64896a791ac1dd6f8b62", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.9", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do good; reap in due season; do not give up", "note": "Doing good is to continue because one will reap in due season without giving up; it closely supplies the commentary's success as remaining in good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-64911aa74fe3ec99d024", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.11", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "training yields peaceful fruit of righteousness", "note": "Discipline is painful at first but later yields peaceful fruit of righteousness to those trained; it gives a secondary purification-to-fruit sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8ea8a5bdf0440e34123", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain-fed land yields useful crop and receives blessing", "note": "Commentary contrasts cultivated yield with failed wild growth; Hebrews says land drinking rain and producing useful crops for cultivators is blessed, making productive reception of rain the positive outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-128b6b853bca370f7c29", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain-soaked land; useful crop; blessing", "note": "Land that has drunk repeated rain produces a crop useful to its cultivators and receives God's blessing; it closely matches the commentary's worked, growing field rather than mere pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b31ea445fde8914fde3a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "thorns yield curse and burning", "note": "Against the commentary's cultivated crop that is harvested and its failing pasture, Hebrews' same rain-fed ground can instead bear thorns, be rejected, and end in burning; it sharpens the field's two outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c8c89e679b4de44f7f1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thorns and thistles; curse and burning", "note": "The contrasting land bears thorns and thistles and approaches curse and burning; it gives an ethical version of the commentary's same-ground contrast between productive cultivation and vegetation headed for ruin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be852d852cf3a3589f91", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.18", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit of righteousness sown in peace", "note": "A fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those who make peace; it gives a concise ethical sowing-and-yield image for the commentary's field of purified good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f48fcfa1b1939aa9ef0a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer waits patiently for precious earth's fruit and early and late rain", "note": "The commentary presents lasting good as the result of crop growth; James makes a farmer's patience for the precious harvest an example of waiting, adding the time required for yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cc6061945f3c1cfd3d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer waits for precious fruit and rains", "note": "The farmer waits patiently for the earth's precious fruit and its rains while believers await the Lord; this connects crop maturation to the commentary's enduring, eschatological good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8f50dfd8fca7a806ca3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.24", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grain that dies bears much fruit", "note": "The commentary sets a fading pasture against growth that yields; Jesus' grain image makes loss precede abundant fruit, a specific but differently focused reversal."}

{"connection_id": "BC-ab6a94370ffc7a70f5b5", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.1", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Father as vinedresser", "note": "Jesus calls the Father the vinedresser; this is a direct Christian agricultural analogue for the commentary's Rabb who maintains and restores a farm."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cbda0ab5be9bea3016f2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "fruit-bearing branch is pruned so it bears more fruit", "note": "The commentary joins purification with crop increase; Jesus uses καθαίρει, a verb for pruning or cleansing, of the already fruitful branch, explicitly meeting purification and increased fruit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a7b13ce49ebb3706e83", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.2", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pruning cleans fruitful branches for more fruit", "note": "The vinedresser removes barren branches and prunes fruit-bearing ones so they bear more; it puts cleansing and maintenance directly inside a growth-to-fruit process."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a875abee73c046c9637d", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.3", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "clean because of the spoken word", "note": "Within the vine and vinedresser discourse, Jesus calls the disciples clean because of his word; it gives a distinct purification claim beside the commentary's z-k-w cleansing within cultivated growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-825749384a95388bd3bb", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unabiding branch withers and is burned", "note": "A branch that does not remain withers and is gathered for fire; it supplies a pointed vegetal reverse to the commentary's crop that remains in good rather than becoming ruined growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8603491b083f64c8ced", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.36", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "harvested fruit for eternal life", "note": "The reaper gathers fruit for eternal life, so sower and reaper rejoice together; it makes harvest an image of the enduring good emphasized at 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ba07de591424b4c528b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.12", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gathering leftovers so nothing is lost", "note": "After all have eaten, Jesus orders the remaining fragments gathered so nothing is lost; this is a secondary food analogue for the commentary's baqī, the yield that remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fa77d10be5c94913bb4", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.27", "target": "sec6", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishing food and food enduring to eternal life", "note": "Jesus contrasts food that perishes with food enduring to eternal life; it maps the commentary's food-and-field imagery onto its world-life and lasting-afterlife contrast."}
